{
 "id": "2025-zhang-urban",
 "name": "Urban Green Space Plantscape Beauty (SBE rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Zhang, Gao, Ren, Ge, Wang, Du, Liu & Kong (2025), \"Evaluation of urban green space plant landscape quality in Zhengzhou city using the AHP-SBE method\", PLOS ONE 20(9): e0329119. Participants rate the scenic beauty of 40 photographs of planted landscape units in Zhengzhou, eight from each green-space type (GY park, FH protective, GC square, FS affiliated, QY regional), on the paper's 7-point scale (1 extremely unattractive … 7 extremely beautiful). Each photo is shown for 10 s in the paper's fixed order; the next one appears when 10 s have passed and a score is given. A background question records whether the participant studies or works in gardening, horticulture or landscape architecture (the paper recruited only lay adults). Answers are keyed by plot code; standardise per rater and average per photo to get SBE values. Original result (30 lay adults): only 17 of 40 plots scored above zero; park green spaces had the highest mean SBE (0.29) and regional green spaces the lowest (−0.43); GY7, GC1, GY2, FS1 and FS5 ranked top; SBE agreed with expert AHP scores (Spearman rs = 0.738). Photographs: the 40 plot photos printed in the paper's S1 File (CC BY 4.0), taken by the authors; they are small (about 230 × 170 px). Differences from the original study: (1) self-paced online page instead of a researcher-hosted PowerPoint slide show with 5-s transitions (here 1 s); (2) a photo stays until a score is given, whereas the slide show moved on automatically; (3) photos come from the small grid in the supplementary file, not the original full-resolution files; (4) age, gender and the professional-background question are asked on the page instead of being screened by the recruitment platform.",
 "author": "Zhang et al.",
 "year": "2025",
